# =============================================================================
# air-aspect-decline — declined AIR proof-aspect shapes.
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • a runtime `itera ab` range has no concrete lane facts, so the report
#     emits no UnrollEquivalence obligation (decline paths emit none)
#   • `return a + b` uses binary arithmetic outside the v1 scalar/let/call
#     subset, so the report emits no GatePurity obligation
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • expecting a declined shape to appear as a failed proof — a decline is
#     silence, not a NotProven verdict
#
# See also: radix-lane, itera, ab, air-aspect-admit
# =============================================================================

# Declaration-only proof fixture: the aspect report inspects these functions;
# this source intentionally has no executable entry point.
#
# Declined Line 1 shape: the range bounds are runtime values, so no concrete
# lanes exist and no unroll obligation is recorded.
fn deambulatio(int n) → int {
    const int summa ← 0
    for range n ‥ 4 const i {
    }
    return summa
}

# Declined Line 3 shape: binary arithmetic is outside the v1 purity subset.
fn additio(int a, int b) → int {
    return a + b
}
